    "body": "### Problem\n\nAs in the title.\n\nSome explanation why `Ignore` group should be hidden.\n\n1. the documentation explicitly says so:\n\n    > (if syntax highlighting works properly you can see the actual color, except for \"Ignore\"):\n    > ...\n    > Ignore                left blank, hidden  `hl-Ignore`\n    > \n    >                                      **hl-Ignore**\n    > When using the Ignore group, you may also consider using the conceal mechanism.  See `conceal`.\n\n2. this is vim's default behavior.\n3. If Ignore and Normal is the same, there wouldn't be any value in using Ignore, the user could just use Normal instead.\n\nThis could be \"workaround\" by adding `highlight Ignore ctermfg=0 guifg=bg` somewhere in vimrc, but ideally the default is reasonable.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nRun `:highlight Ignore`. Observe `xxx links to Normal`.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nObserve `ctermfg=0 guifg=bg` instead.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nVim shows `ctermfg=0 guifg=bg`.\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nUbuntu 22\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\ngnome-terminal\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nnot relevant\n\n### Installation\n\nsnap",
